# Environmental Factors Affecting **Space Traveler Health**

The primary environmental hazards threatening space traveler health decompose into physical spaceflight forces, closed-habitat atmospheric conditions, and psychosocial isolation stressors.

## Physical and Radiation Hazards

Physical forces in low Earth orbit and deep space systematically alter cellular and organ homeostasis.

* **Microgravity and unloading**: Sustained weightlessness causes progressive **bone mineral loss**, severe muscle atrophy, and cardiovascular deconditioning (Demontis et al., 2017; Blaber et al., 2010; Costa et al., 2021).
* **Galactic cosmic rays**: High linear energy transfer heavy ions traverse spacecraft shielding, precipitating **genotoxic DNA damage** and elevated oncogenic risks (Tomsia et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2022; Trivedi & Prajapati, 2026; Willey et al., 2021).
* **Solar particle events**: Episodic high-energy proton showers impose substantial cumulative exposure, demanding dedicated **emergency storm sheltering** (Tomsia et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2022; Willey et al., 2021).
* **Secondary radiation emission**: Collision of cosmic rays with ISS metal bulkheads produces secondary **neutrons and gamma-rays**, accelerating intracellular oxidative stress (Xu et al., 2022; Kim et al., 2026).
* **Fluid redistribution**: Cephalad fluid shifting triggers intracranial venous engorgement, contributing directly to **neuro-ocular remodeling** and visual acuity deficits (Kim et al., 2026; Freitas et al., 2026; Kapoor et al., 2025).

## Habitat and Life Support Environment

The artificially engineered life-support systems of the ISS introduce internal chemical, mechanical, and microbiological exposures.

* **Carbon dioxide accumulation**: Elevated ambient $\text{CO}_2$ resulting from crew respiration and scrubbing efficiency can induce **headaches and hypercapnia** (Xu et al., 2022; Hincapié, 2022).
* **Volatile organic compounds**: Continuous hardware outgassing and payload operations generate trace chemical contaminants that challenge **cabin air quality** (Desai et al., 2022; Salido et al., 2025; Hincapié, 2022).
* **Acoustic noise and vibration**: Persistent operation of fans, pumps, and structural life-support systems induces **auditory fatigue** and sleep disturbances (Morphew, 2020; Desai et al., 2022; Hincapié, 2022).
* **Altered ambient humidity**: Dry module atmospheres compounded by limited hygiene protocols exacerbate **skin barrier dysfunction**, pruritus, and contact dermatitis (Radstake et al., 2022; Trivedi & Prajapati, 2026).
* **Depleted environmental microbiome**: Rigorous disinfection diminishes environmental microbial diversity, leading to **immune dysregulation** and frequent atypical hypersensitivities (Salido et al., 2025; Winer et al., 2025).

## Psychosocial and Chronobiological Stressors

Confinement within remote spacecraft environments produces chronic neural, behavioral, and immunological strains.

* **Circadian rhythm disruption**: Rapid orbital day-night cycles and artificial habitat lighting lead to **chronic sleep deprivation** and neurocognitive throughput declines (Krittanawong et al., 2022; Desai et al., 2022; Freitas et al., 2026).
* **Confinement and restricted space**: Extreme spatial limitation impairs operational efficiency and increases interpersonal friction during **long-duration space exploration** (Morphew, 2020; Kim et al., 2026; Costa et al., 2021).
* **Social isolation**: Protracted separation from terrestrial familial networks induces persistent **psychosocial stress** that alters host neurochemical and endocrine pathways (Morphew, 2020; Tomsia et al., 2024; Desai et al., 2022).
* **Hypomagnetic field exposure**: Transitioning outside terrestrial boundary conditions diminishes geomagnetic protection, potentially impacting **central neural processes** and vascular regulation (Xu et al., 2022; Hart, 2023).
* **Opportunistic pathogen reactivation**: Synergistic physiological and psychological operational stressors provoke subclinical immunosuppression, precipitating **latent herpesvirus reactivation** (Krittanawong et al., 2022; Salido et al., 2025; Winer et al., 2025; Crucian et al., 2020).

These multisystem environmental factors interact dynamically, requiring integrated nutritional, physical, and operational countermeasures to preserve astronaut performance and long-term vitality (Kim et al., 2026; Crucian et al., 2020; Kapoor et al., 2025).
 
